<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Fractals on 0x32++</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/tags/fractals/</link><description>Recent content in Fractals on 0x32++</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Sat, 14 Nov 2026 14:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://blog.0x32.co.uk/tags/fractals/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>The Fractal Zoom Video, Now in the Repo</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/fractal-zoom-video-script/</link><pubDate>Sat, 14 Nov 2026 14:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/fractal-zoom-video-script/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Yesterday&amp;rsquo;s &lt;a href="https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/retro-fractal-zoom/"&gt;retro fractal zoom&lt;/a&gt; has a proper home now. &lt;code&gt;zoom_video.py&lt;/code&gt; lives in the &lt;code&gt;python/&lt;/code&gt; folder of my &lt;a href="https://github.com/alastairhm/fractals"&gt;fractals&lt;/a&gt; repo, along with the scripts from &lt;a href="https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/more-fractals-explorer-and-python/"&gt;the last update&lt;/a&gt;. It can spin the view and zoom back out again, and it runs a fair bit faster than it did.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="what-it-draws"&gt;What it draws&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;It dives from the whole Mandelbrot set down to the spot used by the old Andromeda Research demo, −1.74747070750 + 0.00087953063i, at 11.1 million times magnification. It only draws the &lt;em&gt;edges&lt;/em&gt; between iteration bands and leaves the bands themselves black. Far from the set the bands are wide, so you get thin contour lines; close in they crowd together into solid colour. Each frame is rendered at 640×480 and doubled up with nearest-neighbour scaling, which keeps the chunky VGA feel.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>More Fractals: An Interactive Explorer and Some Python</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/more-fractals-explorer-and-python/</link><pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 22:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/more-fractals-explorer-and-python/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://github.com/alastairhm/fractals"&gt;fractals&lt;/a&gt; repo from my &lt;a href="https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/vibe-coding-pascal-fractals/"&gt;Pascal Mandelbrot post&lt;/a&gt; didn&amp;rsquo;t stay still for long. There&amp;rsquo;s a fifth Pascal program you can explore with, and some Python scripts that draw other kinds of fractals in a retro style.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="mandelbrot5-an-interactive-explorer"&gt;mandelbrot5: an interactive explorer&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;mandelbrot4&lt;/code&gt; could pan and zoom, but only by rerunning it with new numbers. &lt;code&gt;mandelbrot5&lt;/code&gt; turns it into an explorer you drive with the keyboard. It fills the terminal and follows it when you resize it, and every frame is redrawn in place.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Retro Fractal Zoom</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/retro-fractal-zoom/</link><pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 09:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/retro-fractal-zoom/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;This one tickled my 8 bit brain.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;I remember having to leave my BBC Micro on overnight to calculate a single low res image of a Mandelbrot fractal. Now, with a couple of prompts and a bit of to and fro with the Claude app on my phone, it renders a whole retro styled zoom video like this in a couple of minutes.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div style="top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; height: 0; position: relative; padding-bottom: 75%;"&gt;&#10;&lt;iframe style="width: 100%; height: 100%; position: absolute;" src="https://embedr.flickr.com/photos/55649516648" width="960" height="720" frameborder="0" allow="autoplay; fullscreen" allowfullscreen title="Retro fractal zoom"&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.flickr.com/photos/doodle_m/55649516648/"&gt;Retro fractal zoom on Flickr&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Vibe Coding Mandelbrots in Pascal</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/vibe-coding-pascal-fractals/</link><pubDate>Tue, 10 Nov 2026 21:30:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/vibe-coding-pascal-fractals/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;A bit of evening fun: I wanted to see how far a quick vibe coding session could take a Mandelbrot renderer in &lt;a href="https://www.freepascal.org/"&gt;Free Pascal&lt;/a&gt;, drawing straight into the terminal. Pascal was one of the first languages I used, and the Mandelbrot set is the classic &amp;ldquo;hello world&amp;rdquo; of fractals, so it seemed like a good pairing.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;The result is &lt;a href="https://github.com/alastairhm/fractals"&gt;alastairhm/fractals&lt;/a&gt;: four small programs, each one building on the one before, going from plain ASCII art up to truecolor half-block graphics.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Fractals</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/fractals/</link><pubDate>Tue, 10 May 2016 21:45:00 +0100</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/fractals/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src='https://s-media-cache-ak0.pinimg.com/236x/21/c2/ef/21c2ef7d84bd3b530423effdffaa59da.jpg' style='max-width:586px;' /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div&gt;Pinterest Find via Fractals http://ift.tt/27bGq6B&lt;/div&gt;</description></item><item><title>Erik Söderberg, Frac</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/erik-soderberg-frac/</link><pubDate>Wed, 04 May 2016 11:18:00 +0100</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/erik-soderberg-frac/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src='https://s-media-cache-ak0.pinimg.com/236x/38/f8/9d/38f89d904f3d120e6531affb06d78aa6.jpg' style='max-width:586px;' /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div&gt;Pinterest Find via Fractals http://ift.tt/1rTFN1B&lt;/div&gt;</descri